# 6. Reference Copy

Consider:

```java
Box b1 = new Box();
Box b2 = b1;
```

Here, `b2 = b1` **does not create a new object**.

It copies the **reference**.

Therefore, both variables refer to the same object.

```text
b1 ────┐
       ↓
    Box Object
       ↑
b2 ────┘
```

### Important Points

- One object
- Two reference variables
- Both refer to the same object
- No new object is created by `b2 = b1`

### Example

```java
class Box {
    int value;
}

Box b1 = new Box();
b1.value = 10;

Box b2 = b1;
b2.value = 50;

System.out.println(b1.value);
```

Output:
50

### Why?
Because `b1` and `b2` refer to the **same object**.
> **Reference Copy → Same Object**
---


# 7. Object Copy

An object copy means **creating a new object and copying data into it**.

Example:

```java
class Box {
    int value;
}

Box b1 = new Box();
b1.value = 10;

Box b2 = new Box();
b2.value = b1.value;
```

Now there are two separate objects:

```text
b1 ────> Box Object 1
          value = 10

b2 ────> Box Object 2
          value = 10
```

If:

```java
b2.value = 20;

System.out.println(b1.value);
System.out.println(b2.value);
```

Output:
10
20
```

### Why?

Because `b1` and `b2` refer to **different objects**.

> **Object Copy → Separate Objects**

---

# 8. Reference Copy vs Object Copy

| Reference Copy      |    Object Copy |
| Reference is copied | Data is copied |
| No new object is created | New object is created |
| Both references refer to the same object | References refer to different objects |
| Changes affect the same object | Changes are independent |





null Reference

`Definition`

null is a special literal in Java that represents the absence of an object
reference.

A reference variable assigned null does not currently refer to an object.

class Student {
    String name;
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = null;

        System.out.println(s1);
    }
}

output:- null



Accessing a null reference


Student s1 = null;
System.out.println(s1.name);


This throws a NullPointerException at runtime because s1 does not refer
to an object.



Student s1 = null;
System.out.println(s1.name);


This throws a NullPointerException at runtime because s1 does not refer
to an object.

Assigning an object later


Student s1 = null;

s1 = new Student();
s1.name = "Deepak";

System.out.println(s1.name);


Output:  Deepak


Now s1 refers to an actual object.

Important points
- null is not an object.
- null is not the same as zero.
- null is not an empty string.
- Reference variables can be assigned null.
- Dereferencing null causes a NullPointerException.

Difference between null, 0, and ""
Value      Meaning
null       No object reference
0          Integer value zero
""        An empty String object


