--- ### Passing Objects to Methods in Java  ----

Objects can be passed as arguments to methods.

Example
```
class Student {
    String name;
}

public class Main {
    static void changeName(Student s) {
        s.name = "Rahul";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student student = new Student();
        student.name = "Deepak";

        changeName(student);

        System.out.println(student.name);
    }
}
```

Output
Rahul
---

## What Happens?

First:
```
Student student = new Student();
student.name = "Deepak";
```

Think:
```
student ───────────> Student Object
                       name = "Deepak"
```

Then we call:
```
changeName(student);
```

Java **passes a copy of the reference** to the method.

So now:
```text
student ───────────> Student Object
                       name = "Deepak"
                          ↑
                          │
s ────────────────────────┘
```

Both `student` and `s` refer to the **same Student object**.

Therefore:
```java
s.name = "Rahul";
```

changes the same object:

```text
student ───────────> Student Object
                       name = "Rahul"
                          ↑
                          │
s ────────────────────────┘
```

So:
```java
System.out.println(student.name);
```

prints:  Rahul

---

# The Most Important Point

### Java is ALWAYS pass-by-value.
When an object is passed to a method:
> **Java copies the reference value, not the object itself.**

So:
```text
Object → NOT copied
Reference → COPIED
```

---
# Why Can the Method Change the Object?

Because both references point to the **same object**.
```text
student ──┐
          ↓
      [Student Object]
          ↑
s ────────┘
```
Therefore:
```java
s.name = "Rahul";
```

changes the object that `student` also refers to.

---

# Very Important Difference

### Changing the object's field
```java
s.name = "Rahul";
```
✅ Changes the original object.


### Changing the reference
```java
s = new Student();
```
❌ Does NOT change `student`.

Why?
Because `s` is only a **local copy of the reference**.

```text
Before:
student ──→ Object 1
             ↑
s ───────────┘

After: s = new Student();

student ──→ Object 1
s ────────→ Object 2
```

`student` still points to Object 1.

---

# Easy Memory Trick

Remember this:

> **REFERENCE IS COPIED, OBJECT IS NOT COPIED.**

```text
Pass object to method
        ↓
Reference gets copied
        ↓
Both references → Same Object
        ↓
Change object's field
        ↓
Original object changes
```

But:

```text
s = new Student()
        ↓
Only local reference changes
        ↓
Original reference is unchanged
```

---

# Interview Answer
> **Java is always pass-by-value. When an object is passed to a method,
a copy of the object's reference is passed. Therefore, the method can
modify the object's fields, but it cannot change the caller's reference
to point to a different object.**

### One-line rule
> **Copy the reference → same object.
Change the reference → only local reference changes.**


