4.10 Constructor Chaining
`Definition`
Constructor chaining means calling one constructor from another constructor
to reuse initialization logic.

It can happen:
- Within the same class using this().
- In a subclass using super() to invoke a superclass constructor.

1. Imagine you are creating a student record system.
Every student has:
- Name
- Age
- City
Suppose most students are from Ahmedabad, so if someone doesn't provide a city, you want to set it to "Ahmedabad" automatically.
You want to create students in three ways:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student("Deepak");
Student s3 = new Student("Rahul", 22);


The expected  result:

Student	 Name	   Age	     City
s1	    Unknown	       0	Ahmedabad
s2	    Deepak	       0	Ahmedabad
s3	    Rahul	       22	Ahmedabad


Without constructor chaining
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;
    String city;

    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
        city = "Ahmedabad";
    }

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        age = 0;
        city = "Ahmedabad";
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        city = "Ahmedabad";
    }
}


Look at the city variable.
We write this line three times:
city = "Ahmedabad";


Why? Because all three constructors need to initialize the city.

Now imagine your application has 20 constructors and you decide to change
the default city from Ahmedabad to Mumbai.

You may need to update the same line in 20 places.

If you forget even one, your program might behave inconsistently.
That's unnecessary repetition.

That's unnecessary repetition.

3. With constructor chaining
Instead of repeating the same code, we create one constructor that does
all the actual initialization.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;
    String city;

    Student() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    Student(String name) {
        this(name, 0);
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.city = "Ahmedabad";
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name + " | Age: " + age + " | City: " + city);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();
        Student s2 = new Student("Deepak");
        Student s3 = new Student("Rahul", 22);

        System.out.println("--- Student Details ---");
        s1.display();
        s2.display();
        s3.display();
    }
}

output:-
--- Student Details ---
Name: Unknown | Age: 0 | City: Ahmedabad
Name: Deepak | Age: 0 | City: Ahmedabad
Name: Rahul | Age: 22 | City: Ahmedabad

           How the shared logic works
                 Student()
              Passes Unknown, 0

             Student(String name)
              Passes Deepak, 0
                   |
                   ^
              Common constructor
             Student(String name, int age)

             this.city = "Ahmedabad";

So, why do we need constructor chaining?
Think of it this way:
- Without constructor chaining: each constructor does its own initialization, even if some of that work is identical.
- With constructor chaining: one constructor performs the common work, while other constructors pass the necessary information to it.


