# 4.14 Difference Between `this()` and `super()`

| `this()`                        | `super()` |
| Calls another constructor       | Calls a constructor of the immediate  |
  in the same class.                parent class.

| Used for same-class constructor | Used for superclass constructor  |
  chaining.                          chaining.

| Passes arguments to another     | Passes arguments to a parent class |
  constructor in the current class. constructor.

| Must be the first statement when | Must be the first statement when  |
  explicitly used.                  explicitly used under traditional constructor rules.

| Cannot be explicitly combined with| Cannot be explicitly combined with |
  `super()` in the same constructor. `this()` in the same constructor.

### Example of `super()`

```java
class Animal {

    String name;

    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("Parent constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    String breed;

    Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);
        this.breed = breed;
        System.out.println("Child constructor");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog("Tommy", "Labrador");
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Parent constructor
Child constructor
```

### Explanation

1. `new Dog("Tommy", "Labrador")` calls the child constructor.
2. `super(name)` invokes the parent constructor `Animal(String name)`.
3. The parent constructor initializes the `name` field.
4. After the parent constructor completes, execution continues in the child constructor.
5. The child constructor initializes `breed`.

Here, `super(name)` is used to initialize the parent class portion of the object, while `this.breed` refers to the child object's instance variable.

