## 1. final Keyword in Java
The final keyword in Java is used to restrict modifications in different contexts:

Final variables (constant values)
Final methods (prevents method overriding)
Final classes (prevents inheritance)


Final Variables
A final variable must be initialized once and cannot be changed after initialization.

Example 1: Final variable with a primitive type

class Example {
    final int MAX_LIMIT = 100; // Must be initialized
    void display() {
        // MAX_LIMIT = 200; // ❌ Compilation error (cannot modify final variable)
        System.out.println("Max Limit: " + MAX_LIMIT);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Example obj = new Example();
        obj.display();
    }
}

Output:
Max Limit: 100

 Explanation: The MAX_LIMIT variable is declared as final, so its value cannot be modified after assignment.


####  Final Reference Variables (Objects)
  A final reference variable must always refer to the same object, but the object's contents can still be modified.

  Example 2: Final reference type

  class Student {
      int age = 18;
  }

  class Example {
      final Student student = new Student(); // Final reference variable

      void changeValues() {
          student.age = 20; // ✅ Allowed (modifying object content)
          // student = new Student(); // ❌ Compilation error (cannot reassign reference)
      }
  }

  public class Main {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Example obj = new Example();
          obj.changeValues();
          System.out.println("Student age: " + obj.student.age);
      }
  }

Output:
Student age: 20

Explanation: The final reference cannot point to another object, but the internal properties of the object can still change.




# Final Methods
  A final method cannot be overridden by a subclass.

  Example 3: Final method

class Parent {
    final void show() {
        System.out.println("This is a final method.");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    // void show() { // ❌ Compilation error (Cannot override final method)
    //     System.out.println("Trying to override final method");
    // }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child obj = new Child();
        obj.show(); // Calls the method from Parent class
    }
}


Output:
This is a final method.
🔹 Explanation: The show() method in Parent is final, so it cannot be overridden in Child


# Final Classes
  A final class cannot be extended (inherited) by another class.


final class Vehicle {
    void run() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle is running.");
    }
}

// class Car extends Vehicle { // ❌ Compilation error (Cannot inherit from final class)
// }

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Vehicle();
        v.run();
    }
}

Output:
Vehicle is running.

🔹 Explanation: The Vehicle class is marked as final, so no other class can extend it.

Do not use finalize() for cleanup. Instead, use try-with-resources and AutoCloseable for resource management


## . Why We Don't Use new Keyword for Creating Primitive Data Types?

The new keyword is used to allocate memory dynamically for objects, but
primitive data types (int, char, double, etc.) are stored in stack memory, making them more efficient.

Reasons:

Efficiency – Creating primitives with new would be slower.
Memory Optimization – Primitive variables take less memory compared to objects.
Direct Storage – Primitive types are directly stored in stack memory instead of heap.


int a = 10; // Efficient (stored in stack memory)

// Not recommended:
Integer b = new Integer(10); // Uses heap memory (wrapper class)






4. Memory Allocation of new Keyword
When we use new, memory is allocated in the heap, and the reference is stored in the stack.

Example:
class Person {
    String name;
    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}


Person p1 = new Person("John"); // "John" stored in heap, reference in stack.

Memory Areas in Java:

Heap – Stores objects created using new.
Stack – Stores method calls and local primitive variables.
Method Area – Stores class metadata, static variables, and method code.
PC Register & Native Stack – Stores program counters and native method info





5. Wrapper Classes
Wrapper classes convert primitive data types into objects (Boxing) and vice versa (Unboxing).
Primitive Type                        Wrapper Class
byte	                               Byte
short	                              Short
int	                                  Integer
long	                                Long
float	                             Float
double	                               Double
char	                            Character
boolean	                           Boolean

Integer num = 10; // Auto-boxing (int → Integer)
int n = num; // Auto-unboxing (Integer → int)

Why Use Wrapper Classes?

Collections (like ArrayList) require objects, not primitives.
Supports features like null values, synchronization, and utility methods.




# 7. Garbage Collection in Java
Garbage Collection (GC) is an automatic memory management feature in Java that removes unused objects to free memory.

How It Works?

Objects without references are eligible for GC.
JVM runs GC automatically, but we can request it using System.gc().
GC uses algorithms like Mark and Sweep, Reference Counting, and Generational GC.
Example:

class Test {
    protected void finalize() {
        System.out.println("Object is being garbage collected");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test obj1 = new Test();
        obj1 = null; // Eligible for GC
        System.gc(); // Request GC (Not guaranteed to run immediately)
    }
}
Advantages:

Prevents memory leaks.
Improves application performance by reclaiming memory.
